# gtm-ci

The owner builds this repo herself, one task from `TASKS.md` at a time, in Cursor on a Windows laptop (PowerShell, VS Code terminal). Explain every change in plain, simple language.

## Source of truth

- Follow `docs/spec.md` and treat it as the source of truth.
- If a task and the spec disagree, follow the spec and say so.
- Facts about Deepline come from spec section 3 (verified facts, with URLs). If you need a Deepline fact that is not there, check the live docs (https://deepline.com/docs/llms.txt) or `deepline <command> --help`, and say where it came from. Do not guess a CLI flag, a tool id, a price, or an output shape.

## Stack

- Python 3.11 for the scorer, the CI glue, and the tests. Use the standard library plus pytest. Do not add a package unless the task says so.
- TypeScript only where Deepline needs it: the Play (`plays/*.play.ts`), its rules file (`plays/rules.ts`), and the offline runner (`scripts/offline_run.ts`). Run TypeScript with `npx tsx`.
- Node 22, and the `deepline` CLI pinned to the version in the spec.
- Show commands for PowerShell. For JSON input to the CLI, use `--input @file.json`, not inline JSON, because PowerShell quoting breaks inline JSON.

## Cost and tools

- Free or cheap tools only. The only paid thing is Deepline credits, with a total budget of $10 to $20.
- Offline mode is the default. Tests and local runs make no Deepline calls and spend 0 credits.
- A live Deepline run needs: a reason, `--max-credits` set, and a new line in `docs/spend.md` with the date, run id, and the credits from `deepline billing usage --run-id <id> --json`.
- Never buy credits, change a billing cap, or turn on auto-recharge. Only the owner does that.

## Never invent numbers

- Every number in a README, a comment, a learning note, or a post must come from a committed file in `results/` or `docs/spend.md`.
- If a credit number is missing, write "unknown", never 0.
- Do not tune the rules to make the test set score look good. Do not edit an expected answer to match the code. If they disagree, say which one is wrong and why.

## Synthetic data only

- Every person in `fixtures/` is made up (surname `Testcase`, email local part `synthetic.<id>`). Offline companies use reserved example domains.
- Never send a synthetic person, or any real person, to a live Deepline email or person tool.
- No real person's name, email, or profile goes into this repo.

## Standalone

- The project is standalone: no imports, links, or references to any other repo.

## Secrets

- Secrets live in `.env.deepline` or `.env` on the laptop, and in GitHub repository secrets. They are never committed and never printed.
- `.env.example` lists the variable names only.

## No real side effects

- Never send email or messages. Never write to a CRM. Never publish or deploy a Play (`deepline plays publish` or `deepline plays deploy`).
- Never create a GitHub repo, push, open a pull request, or post anything unless the owner asks for that exact step in this task.

## How to work

- Work in small steps, and run `pytest` after each change.
- Skip a task marked cut only when the owner says she is behind, and only in the cut order in `TASKS.md`.

## Code she can read

- The code must be simple and readable: plain functions, clear names, no clever one-liners, no classes unless a task needs one, and no unnecessary abstractions. Add short comments that explain why, not obvious what.
- After every task, explain in plain English what you built, how the pieces connect, and why each decision was made. Point out anything she should be able to explain in an interview.
- Answer the task's "Understand it" questions.
- Append those notes to `docs/LEARNING.md`. Add a few lines. Do not rewrite earlier notes.

## Commits

- Commit messages are all lowercase, short, and plain, the way a person writes them by hand.
- Examples: `add icp rules`, `score false alerts`, `post before after comment`.
- No conventional-commit prefixes like `feat:` or `chore:`, no emoji, no capitals, no long bodies, and no "this commit..." wording.
